1
00:00:04,674 --> 00:00:06,670
Hello, I'm Sangsoo Jeong.

2
00:00:06,671 --> 00:00:13,950
This time, before you start taking the class, I'd like to go over what you should prepare, and talk about what you'll be learning going forward.

3
00:00:13,951 --> 00:00:16,430
Drawing is an incredibly efficient medium for conveying meaning.

4
00:00:17,045 --> 00:00:20,990
You can pack in all sorts of meaning without having to explain everything in words.

5
00:00:20,991 --> 00:00:24,830
Content is being created and piling up on the internet at a tremendous pace, even right now.

6
00:00:25,475 --> 00:00:28,750
It could be writing, videos, drawings, or photos.

7
00:00:28,751 --> 00:00:35,900
Among all of that, the power of an image to make you stop scrolling, even as the page flies by, is remarkable.

8
00:00:35,901 --> 00:00:39,820
It can make you feel a wide range of emotions in just half a second.

9
00:00:39,821 --> 00:00:49,980
In this course, I'll be sharing my own know-how, the drawing skills and theories that go into making images even more attractive, so they can stop your scroll for half a second longer.

10
00:00:54,385 --> 00:00:56,650
First, let me explain what you'll need.

11
00:00:56,651 --> 00:01:00,090
My class uses Photoshop and a tablet.

12
00:01:00,091 --> 00:01:05,290
I use a Wacom Intuos Pro and Photoshop Creative Cloud 2022.

13
00:01:05,290 --> 00:01:10,010
If you don't have the same version, the screen might look a little different, so please keep that in mind.

14
00:01:10,011 --> 00:01:30,731
Other drawing tools like Clip Studio Paint, or tablet PCs like the iPad, won't let you follow along with exactly what I'm doing, but since this is a class for intermediate artists who already have some understanding of drawing, I hope you'll apply the principles and process know-how I explain to your own workflow, without being tied to a specific tool.

15
00:01:30,984 --> 00:01:39,131
For those of you aiming to get hired by a game company, companies usually provide Photoshop, so learning to work in Photoshop here is something I'd recommend as well.

16
00:01:43,004 --> 00:01:45,720
Now let me take a quick look at the curriculum.

17
00:01:45,721 --> 00:01:51,080
In the Welcome chapter, I briefly introduce myself and what we'll be covering.

18
00:01:51,081 --> 00:02:00,920
In Chapter 1, I talk about the role of concept art and illustration, what's important in drawing, and the methods and mindset for studying it.

19
00:02:02,095 --> 00:02:09,880
I'll also walk through my own working process along with references, explain its advantages, and take some time to win you over.

20
00:02:09,881 --> 00:02:14,140
We'll also look at my Photoshop setup and how I operate my brushes.

21
00:02:14,141 --> 00:02:18,780
I'll explain the working environment I've set up and the shortcuts I mainly use.

22
00:02:19,530 --> 00:02:22,140
I'll also share the brushes I use often and how to manage them.

23
00:02:26,095 --> 00:02:28,360
We'll go over that.

24
00:02:28,520 --> 00:02:32,600
Chapter 2 covers efficient and trendy figure drawing.

25
00:02:32,601 --> 00:02:52,920
Rather than textbook material, I'll focus on what you can apply right away: realistic-form-based sketching, simplifying sketches, and a staged coloring process. I'll show you how to draw the body with a sense of three-dimensionality, and how perspective and composition can turn a picture that's technically accurate but somehow boring into one that looks good.

26
00:02:54,410 --> 00:02:57,210
Chapter 3 covers light and color.

27
00:02:57,850 --> 00:03:06,531
Along with the theory of light and color, we'll practice how to actually handle light, and do a demonstration of applying light to a picture.

28
00:03:06,950 --> 00:03:09,450
Chapter 4 starts the concept art.

29
00:03:09,984 --> 00:03:13,930
We'll do brief planning and learn how to use references.

30
00:03:14,359 --> 00:03:18,330
Based on that plan, we'll proceed with sketching and design.

31
00:03:18,331 --> 00:03:21,050
In Chapter 5 we'll do the coloring on top of the sketch.

32
00:03:21,690 --> 00:03:34,330
Through live demonstrations, I'll open up my working process and know-how: line drawing, lighting, coloring methods, design, and so on. And I'll explain it so you get a lot out of each step.

33
00:03:34,331 --> 00:03:37,971
Chapter 6 covers more advanced coloring.

34
00:03:38,540 --> 00:03:48,010
We'll look at how to add shadows, which is essential for realistic pictures, how to express different materials, and I'll show you the finishing process together.

35
00:03:49,285 --> 00:04:00,960
In Chapter 7, I talk about the different ways to color-correct a picture, and how to use Photoshop features to bring a picture to life, along with my own personal tricks.

36
00:04:00,961 --> 00:04:04,520
Chapter 8 is about mindset when it comes to drawing.

37
00:04:05,200 --> 00:04:14,080
I talk about overcoming slumps, talent, how to survive as a professional, and practical working tips.

38
00:04:14,081 --> 00:04:21,079
Digital drawing, especially concept art, is such a red ocean that there are tons of artists competing fiercely.

39
00:04:21,080 --> 00:04:24,840
HR managers say there are plenty of applicants, but no one to actually hire.

40
00:04:25,725 --> 00:04:28,200
Do you need some extraordinary special weapon that sets you apart?

41
00:04:28,201 --> 00:04:32,520
For example, your own unique designs, your own color sense? No, that's not it.

42
00:04:32,790 --> 00:04:35,841
Companies don't want pictures with flashy designs and flashy colors.

43
00:04:36,760 --> 00:04:40,840
They want someone who can draw pictures and designs that can blend right into the team.

44
00:04:40,841 --> 00:04:44,600
A design and color that's inoffensive and unremarkable is the best.

45
00:04:44,601 --> 00:04:46,200
Ordinariness can be your weapon.

46
00:04:46,880 --> 00:04:49,200
It's very simple, but it's hard.

47
00:04:49,201 --> 00:04:54,320
I like to compare drawing, concept art in particular, to fried rice at a Chinese restaurant.

48
00:04:54,321 --> 00:05:00,240
It's a very plain, simple dish: you get the oil fragrant, then stir-fry egg, vegetables, and rice.

49
00:05:00,241 --> 00:05:04,240
But depending on the chef's skill, the taste varies enormously.

50
00:05:04,770 --> 00:05:09,280
To survive among the countless Chinese restaurants, what do you need to do?

51
00:05:09,281 --> 00:05:12,320
Do you have to make your own unique fried rice?

52
00:05:12,321 --> 00:05:13,600
Should you mix in raisins?

53
00:05:13,601 --> 00:05:15,440
Should you mix in blue food coloring?

54
00:05:16,010 --> 00:05:18,810
That's not what customers want.

55
00:05:18,811 --> 00:05:22,051
When they order fried rice, they have an expected taste and look.

56
00:05:23,030 --> 00:05:24,680
Should you make really good black bean sauce?

57
00:05:25,165 --> 00:05:29,880
The black bean sauce you put on top of the fried rice can be compared to Photoshop's correction features.

58
00:05:29,881 --> 00:05:33,041
Even if the fried rice isn't great, it lets you eat it masked by the sauce's flavor.

59
00:05:33,725 --> 00:05:36,721
But no one calls that well-made fried rice.

60
00:05:37,160 --> 00:05:40,920
So what does it actually take to make really good fried rice?

61
00:05:40,921 --> 00:05:49,800
I think it's fresh ingredients, the wrist technique and wok-tossing that comes from practice, precise flame control and salt balance, and a little bit of the chef's own secret.

62
00:05:49,801 --> 00:05:50,840
Drawing is exactly the same.

63
00:05:51,500 --> 00:06:04,460
In drawing, what matters is accurate form, volume, lighting control, the way color changes with light, optimal composition and gesture, and the brushwork that comes from long practice, all of which exist to communicate design and story.

64
00:06:04,865 --> 00:06:06,780
These are what we call the fundamentals.

65
00:06:07,345 --> 00:06:14,220
I'm confident that solid fundamentals, regardless of your style, will greatly raise your value.

66
00:06:14,221 --> 00:06:17,221
The sauce and the decoration are a matter of taste after that.

67
00:06:17,265 --> 00:06:20,620
Once you have the basic recipe, you can make fried rice no matter what.

68
00:06:21,275 --> 00:06:25,540
But the fried rice that turns a restaurant into a famous one is clearly different.

69
00:06:25,541 --> 00:06:33,700
I want to give you a recipe that can help everyone, from beginner cooks to restaurant owners who have no customers and are struggling.

70
00:06:33,701 --> 00:06:36,621
We're going to set off on a journey toward drawing delicious pictures.

71
00:06:38,430 --> 00:06:42,560
This time, we looked at the purpose and meaning of this class.

72
00:06:42,561 --> 00:06:45,881
Next time, I'll talk about how to study.
